{
  "title": "Inspecting a physics-informed Burgers example",
  "template": "elsevier",
  "content": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 1
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Abstract"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "This example organizes the Burgers-equation materials released with the PINNs project. It combines the original paper, author-provided code and reference data, an editable manuscript and a presentation. The displayed fields are supplied reference solutions; this demonstration does not train a PINN."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 1
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "1. Research question"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Physics-informed neural networks approximate a solution while incorporating a differential-equation residual into training. The Burgers problem provides a compact example in which advection, diffusion and a steepening spatial profile can be inspected together [1]."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 1
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "2. Governing equation"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "blockMath",
        "attrs": {
          "latex": "u_t+u u_x-\\frac{0.01}{\\pi}u_{xx}=0,\\quad x\\in[-1,1],\\quad t\\in[0,1]"
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "blockMath",
        "attrs": {
          "latex": "u(0,x)=-\\sin(\\pi x),\\qquad u(t,-1)=u(t,1)=0"
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The conditions above are taken from Eq. (3) of the downloaded Part I paper, PDF page 5. Coordinates and field values are treated as dimensionless in this example."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 1
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "3. Reference data"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The supplied burgers_shock.mat file contains 256 spatial locations and 100 saved times, from t = 0 to t = 0.99. The plotting script reads this file without changing it and draws the space-time field and four time slices."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "image",
        "attrs": {
          "src": "asset:burgers-reference.png",
          "title": "Figure 1. Author-supplied Burgers reference solution and time slices. This is reference data, not a network prediction.",
          "alt": "Burgers reference field and time slices"
        }
      },
